engine/src/cache.rs (~80 LOC incl. unit tests):
```rust
/// One cached file as `ParseCache::iter` yields it (path order).
pub struct CachedFile<'a> { pub path: &'a str, pub content_hash: u64, pub lang: &'a str, pub parse: &'a FileParse }
/// File-level delta between what the cache holds and one build's inputs. Each list sorted by path.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq, Eq, serde::Serialize)]
pub struct CacheDiff {
    /// In the input with the cached content hash - served from the cache.
    pub reused: Vec<String>,
    /// In the input and either absent from the cache (added) or cached with another hash (modified).
    pub reparsed: Vec<String>,
    /// Cached but absent from the input (deleted, or no longer a main-parser file).
    pub evicted: Vec<String>,
}
impl ParseCache {
    pub fn iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = CachedFile<'_>> + '_;          // BTreeMap order
    pub fn diff(&self, current: &[(String, u64)]) -> CacheDiff;               // pure, no mutation
    pub fn last_diff(&self) -> Option<&CacheDiff>;                            // the diff of the last build that used this cache
    pub(crate) fn record_diff(&mut self, d: CacheDiff);                      // route.rs only
}
```
diff(): walk `current` (duplicates collapse, last wins, then sort) against `entries`: equal hash -> reused, else reparsed; entries not in current -> evicted. It deliberately ignores `lang`: a path's language is a function of its path, and the stamp already invalidates every entry when detection changes. `last_diff: Option<CacheDiff>` is `#[serde(skip)]` (Default = None), so the bincode sidecar layout is unchanged and the stamp check still guards it.
engine/src/route.rs parse_repo_files (~30 LOC): keep `current: Vec<(String, u64)>` - push (path, h) at the cache branch for every file that reaches it; on a miss, collect the fresh parse into `pending: Vec<(String, u64, &str, FileParse)>` instead of calling c.put immediately. After the loop: `let d = c.diff(&current);` (the cache is still the PRE-build state), then apply pending puts, then `c.retain_paths(&live_paths)` exactly as today (a file that failed to reparse is still evicted from the sidecar), then `c.stats = CacheStats { reused: d.reused.len(), reparsed: d.reparsed.len(), evicted: d.evicted.len() }` and `c.record_diff(d)`. The marker keeps its text and gains the source: `[incremental] {repo_label}: reused R, reparsed P, evicted E (parse cache, stamp=<stamp>, source=diff)` - the `(parse cache, stamp=` prefix engine/tests/parser_stamp.rs and humans grep for is unchanged. Semantics note (doc comment on CacheStats): a file whose reparse fails counts as reparsed (the diff describes inputs; the failure is in parse_errors), which is the only way the new numbers can differ from HEAD's loop counters.
TESTS: cache.rs unit tests `diff_classifies_reused_reparsed_evicted` (entries a,b,c; current a (same), b (new hash), d -> {[a],[b,d],[c]}) and `iter_is_path_ordered`; engine/tests/cache_diff.rs (tempfile + generate_one_with_cache, ~60 LOC) `incremental_build_records_the_file_diff`: build a.py b.py c.py -> last_diff {[], [a,b,c], []}; edit b, delete c, add d -> rebuild -> last_diff == {reused:[a.py], reparsed:[b.py, d.py], evicted:[c.py]}, cache.stats equals the three lengths, iter() paths == [a.py, b.py, d.py] and each content_hash == repo_graph_engine::cache::content_hash(<file text>).

