packets.implementation:
FACADE change: graph/src/lib.rs gains `mod nav;` and `pub use nav::{is_nav_route, nav_route_path};` (additive Rust API).
THE LINK CONTRACT every emitter (LA.6b/6c/6d) follows, documented once in nav.rs's module doc and cross-referenced from the NAVIGATES_TO doc line L0.1 writes:
```rust
UnresolvedRef { from: <MODULE id of the linking file | nav ROUTE id for a redirect>, from_module: <MODULE id of the file>,
  qualifier: CallQualifier::Bare(<link>), category: edge_category::NAVIGATES_TO }
// <link> = "/path"        router-API link (routerLink, <Link to>, navigate(), router.push, redirectTo, origin share link): can be DEAD
// <link> = "href:/path"   plain <a href> / location.href assignment: resolves like any link but is NEVER reported dead
//                          (an SPA anchor may legitimately target a server path: OAuth start, logout, a download)
// dynamic segments are written `${...}`; query and fragment are stripped by the emitter
```
CARRIER DESIGN CHOICE. (a) A new `CallQualifier::NavPath` variant - REJECTED: CallQualifier is rkyv-archived inside the persisted `unresolved_refs` (store/src/lib.rs Container), so a variant is an on-disk layout change behind LC.1, and every exhaustive match on CallQualifier (graph/src/calls.rs resolve_calls + resolve_refs, the python/ruby intra-file resolvers) grows an arm. (b) A NAV_LINK node per link site - REJECTED: no node_kind id is allocated to this group, and it adds one node per `<Link>`. (c) `Bare(link)` with the category as discriminator and an `href:` prefix for the plain-anchor tier - CHOSEN: zero format change, one string convention in one module.

graph/src/nav.rs (~230 LOC incl. doc and unit tests):
```rust
pub fn is_nav_route(cells: &[Cell]) -> bool;                // moved verbatim from resolvers/http.rs, now public
/// The request path of a ROUTE qname in every shape the tree emits: owner split first (LB.4a ` @<root>`),
/// then `page:<p>` (LB.4c nav pages), `route:<p>` (go / ts_routes), `<METHOD> <p>` (legacy). None otherwise.
pub fn nav_route_path(qname: &str) -> Option<&str>;
enum Seg { Lit(String), Param }
enum CatchAll { Root, Scoped(Vec<String>) }                // Scoped holds the literal prefix segments
struct NavIndex { exact: HashMap<String, Vec<NodeId>>, by_len: HashMap<usize, Vec<(Vec<Seg>, NodeId)>>, root_catchall: Vec<NodeId>, scoped: Vec<(Vec<String>, NodeId)>, server_paths: HashSet<String> }
#[derive(Default)] pub(crate) struct NavStats { links, exact, param, scoped, suffix, dead, catchall, server, no_router, href_unmatched, orphan, lifted: usize }
pub(crate) fn resolve_nav_links(g: &mut RepoGraph, refs: &[UnresolvedRef]) -> NavStats;
pub(crate) fn lift_nav_endpoints(g: &mut RepoGraph) -> usize;
```
INDEX: iterate `g.nodes` in vec order (deterministic). A ROUTE (`g.nav.kind_by_id`) with `is_nav_route(&n.cells)` -> `nav_route_path(qname)` -> `norm = normalise_http_path(path)`. CATCH-ALL classification on the RAW path: a last segment of `**`, `*`, `[...x]`, `[[...x]]` or any `:name*` / `:name(.*)*` segment is a wildcard. If every segment before it is empty (the whole path is the wildcard: Angular `**`, React `*`, Vue `/:pathMatch(.*)*`) it is a ROOT catch-all: pushed to `root_catchall`, never a match target (Angular's ubiquitous `{ path: '**' }` must not absorb dead links). Otherwise it is SCOPED (Next `pages/docs/[...slug]` -> `/docs/:slug*`, Angular `docs/**`): pushed to `scoped` with its literal prefix. Every ROUTE WITHOUT the nav ORIGIN contributes `norm` to `server_paths`. If LB.4a's owner segment is present, candidates whose owner equals the linking file's owner (derived from the linking MODULE's ROUTE siblings the same way) are preferred before falling back to all.
MATCH one link: strip an `href:` prefix (remember the tier), strip any `?...`/`#...` tail, `norm = normalise_http_path(raw)`. Tier EXACT: `exact[norm]` -> Confidence::Strong. Tier PARAM: equal segment count, a route `Param` matches any link segment, a link `{}` matches only a route `Param` -> Medium. Tier SCOPED: the link's leading segments equal a scoped catch-all's literal prefix and the link has at least one more segment (`[[...x]]` optional catch-alls also match the bare prefix) -> Medium. Tier SUFFIX: the route's segments are a proper suffix of the link's, at least one `Lit` compared, route is not `/` -> Weak (child tables whose parent prefix is in another file, Angular `loadChildren`). First tier with any hit wins; each hit is one edge; dedupe on `(from, to)` with a HashSet so `/users/1` and `/users/${...}` from one page give one edge. The plain-anchor tier caps confidence at Medium. Edges carry the tier confidence (build the Edge directly; calls.rs `push_edge` always writes Strong).
UNMATCHED: index has no nav route -> drop, `no_router += 1` (a server-rendered repo's links are unjudgeable, not dead). `norm` in `server_paths` -> drop, `server += 1`. `from` not a node of `g` (`g.nav.kind_by_id` lookup) -> drop, `orphan += 1`. `href:` tier -> drop, `href_unmatched += 1`. Otherwise `g.unresolved_refs.push(r.clone())` - THAT is the dead-link record, persisted through the store's Container `unresolved_refs` and restored by load_from_gmap; `dead += 1`, and `catchall += 1` too when the index holds a ROOT catch-all (the link lands on the 404/redirect route at runtime).
LIFT (`lift_nav_endpoints`, after resolution): a page is a component, not a file. PAGE COMPONENT = a node whose kind is COMPONENT or whose LB.3 roles (`roles_in(kind, &cells)`) include COMPONENT. For every NAVIGATES_TO edge whose `from` is a MODULE, every HANDLED_BY edge whose `from` is a nav ROUTE and whose `to` is a MODULE, and every NAVIGATES_TO entry in `unresolved_refs` whose `from` is a MODULE: if the DISTINCT page components among `g.nav.children_of[module]` number exactly one, rewrite that endpoint to it (`lifted += 1`); otherwise leave the MODULE (a file with two pages is ambiguous). After the rewrite, drop duplicate (from, to, category) edges among the rewritten ones only (untouched edges keep their bytes and order). Page flow is then page -NAVIGATES_TO-> ROUTE -HANDLED_BY-> page end to end.
calls.rs:resolve_refs: `let (nav_refs, other): (Vec<UnresolvedRef>, Vec<UnresolvedRef>) = refs.iter().cloned().partition(|r| r.category == edge_category::NAVIGATES_TO);` then the existing loop over `&other`; after it `let mut s = crate::nav::resolve_nav_links(g, &nav_refs); s.lifted = crate::nav::lift_nav_endpoints(g);` and print the marker when `s.links > 0`. The partition keeps the non-nav refs in their original order, so every other edge list is byte-identical.
MARKER (once per graph with at least one NAVIGATES_TO ref; `resolved` counts matched REFS, before (from,to) dedupe): `[nav] links=L resolved=R (exact=E param=P scoped=C suffix=S) dead=D catchall=A server=V no_router=N href_unmatched=H orphan=O lifted=F`.
TESTS graph/tests/nav_links.rs (public API only: `build_typescript`, `FileParse`, `RepoGraph` fields, `blast_carry_edges`, `nav_route_path`; its own binary like graph/tests/http_nav_route.rs so no sibling's test file is shared). Nav ROUTE qnames in the setup use the shape the nav emitters produce when this lands (read parsers/code/extractors/src/react.rs: `page:<path>` after LB.4c, else `GET <path>`); every assertion compares route PATHS through `nav_route_path`, never a qname literal. Setup: one FileParse with MODULE `src::App` whose sole page component child is `src::App::Nav` (a FUNCTION carrying LB.3's ROLE cell COMPONENT); nav ROUTEs `/dashboard`, `/users/:id`, `/docs/:slug*` and `/**` each with ROUTE_METHOD + ORIGIN `{"provenance":"nav_route"}`; a server ROUTE `route:/auth/google` without ORIGIN; MODULE `src::Dashboard` with sole page component `src::Dashboard::Dashboard` and an edge HANDLED_BY `/dashboard` -> `src::Dashboard`; NAVIGATES_TO refs from `src::App`: `/dashboard`, `/users/${...}`, `/users/42`, `/docs/intro/start`, `/billing`, `/auth/google`, `href:/logout`, plus one ref whose `from` is a NodeId absent from the graph. Unit test in nav.rs: `nav_route_path_parses_every_shape` (`page:/x`, `GET /x`, `route:/x`, `page:/x @web`).

