PARITY RULES the index must honour (each is a HEAD behaviour a naive CSR would change): (1) DANGLING endpoints - HEAD walks edges by id, so an edge to an id that is no node is still followed and its target reported (TS heritage fabricates such ids until Batch C A6.3); the dense index = node_ids first, then every kept edge endpoint not yet indexed, appended in first-seen edge order. (2) NEIGHBOUR ORDER - HEAD scans edges in global order, so first-reach `via` and queue order depend on it: each incidence list keeps global edge order. (3) Walk::Both - HEAD checks `from == node` then `to == node` per edge in ONE pass (graph/src/blast.rs:111-117), so the out- and in-lists are merged by edge index (two-pointer merge) and an edge on both lists (a self-loop) is taken once, as forward. (4) Duplicate ids in node_ids index once (first occurrence). Only index lookups touch the HashMap - never its iteration order.

