PAIRINGS (each side's fields = its SCHEMA_FIELDS cell; missing on either side -> status unknown, never a guess): schema_copy - every SHARES_SCHEMA edge between two MESSAGE_TYPE nodes (producer = edge.from, consumer = edge.to; the pair is symmetric, so rules that care about direction are evaluated both ways and reported per direction only when they differ); topic - each paired message_contracts row whose producer and consumer message_type resolve to a MESSAGE_TYPE in their own repo (match on the last `.`-segment of the qualified name, unique within the repo, else unknown) - skipped when both resolve to the same NodeId (one shared file: identical by construction); channel - AsyncAPI publish op vs subscribe op with the same normalised channel in different files; route - an OpenAPI op (provider) and a Pact op (consumer) with the same METHOD and normalise_http_path(path).
RULES (the format's own compatibility rules - FACT-level statements about declared schemas; the pairing is DERIVED): proto - same number, different type -> `proto_wire_type` breaking; same name, different number -> `proto_number_changed` breaking; same number, different name -> `proto_renamed` compatible; field only on one side -> `proto_unknown_field` compatible; a number reserved on one side and used on the other -> `proto_reserved_reused` breaking. avro (consumer = reader) - reader field absent in writer without default -> `avro_reader_field_no_default` breaking; writer-only field -> `avro_writer_field_ignored` compatible; type change -> `avro_promotion` compatible for the spec promotions (int->long/float/double, long->float/double, float->double, string<->bytes) else `avro_type_changed` breaking. openapi vs pact - pact request key absent from the provider request schema -> `undeclared_request_field` compatible (the provider may ignore or reject it: reported, not judged); provider-required request field absent from the pact body -> `missing_required_field` breaking; pact response key absent from the provider's schema for that status -> `consumer_expects_undeclared_field` breaking. asyncapi - subscriber field absent from the publisher payload -> `consumer_field_not_produced` breaking; publisher-only -> `producer_extra_field` compatible; type differs -> `type_changed` breaking. status = breaking if any change is breaking, compatible if any change, identical otherwise. Rows sorted by (pairing, key, producer file, consumer file).
Marker: `[contract-fields] pairs=<P> schema_copy=<S> topic=<T> channel=<C> route=<R> identical=<I> compatible=<K> breaking=<B> unknown=<U>`.
